WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.888
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:08.010
SPEAKER: As enterprises move
their applications and services

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.785
to the Cloud, developers need
to evaluate and experiment

00:00:10.785 --> 00:00:12.660
with new technologies
and tools to figure out

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:13.920
what works best for them.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:16.079
This evaluation
process includes tasks

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:18.420
like identifying which
Cloud provider to use,

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:22.260
trying out new products, and
learning how to use new APIs.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.510
However, we know from
talking with developers

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:26.850
that this initial evaluation
and setup phase often

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:27.975
takes longer than expected.

00:00:27.975 --> 00:00:30.392
Maybe you run into problems
while getting your development

00:00:30.392 --> 00:00:32.580
environment set up, like
finding the right libraries

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:34.110
and dependencies.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.730
You probably don't
want to spend hours

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.350
trying to install
all the right tools

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:39.808
or trying to figure out why
the code works on your friend's

00:00:39.808 --> 00:00:42.060
machine, but not yours.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.250
Even after setup,
switching back and forth

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:48.240
between documentation, your
IDE, your terminal window,

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:51.510
and your Cloud
console can get messy.

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:54.540
This is where Cloud Shell
Editor comes into play.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.790
Cloud Shell provides you
with command line access

00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:00.180
to your Cloud resources
directly from your web browser.

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:01.560
And Cloud Shell
Editor, available

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:05.370
at ide.cloud.google.com,
extends Cloud Shell

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:08.610
with an online
development environment.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.160
Powered by the Eclipse
Theia IDE platform,

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:14.040
Cloud Shell Editor makes it easy
for you to write code directly

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.530
from your web browser and
deploy it to the Cloud.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:18.000
This online
development environment

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.680
comes packed with
powerful features

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.930
to streamline your
development workflows.

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:24.870
First, Cloud Shell Editor
integrates Cloud Code,

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:27.270
a set of IDE plugins that
help you stay productive

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:30.210
when developing
cloud-native applications.

00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:33.000
Cloud Code simplifies Kubernetes
and cloud-run development

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:33.780
workflows.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.870
It allows you to build, deploy,
and debug your application

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.430
in just a couple of clicks.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:41.580
It also comes packaged with
container tools, like minikube,

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.460
Skaffold, Buildpacks, and Jib.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:47.430
And with its API Explorer,
discovering, enabling,

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:51.312
and adding Cloud APIs to
your application is easy.

00:01:51.312 --> 00:01:53.940
Cloud Shell Editor also has
version control and support

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.460
for multiple projects,
making it easy for you

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.610
to collaborate with teammates.

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:00.900
Cloud Shell Editor also
has rich language support

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.790
from many popular
programming languages

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.010
to make it easy for you to
write functional, clean,

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.058
and beautiful code.

00:02:07.058 --> 00:02:09.600
To help you get started, we have
online interactive tutorials

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.820
to get you up to speed.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:14.220
You can try some of our Cloud
Shell Editor Quickstarts

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:17.640
for Google Kubernetes
Engine and Cloud Run.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.770
With Cloud Shell Editor,
we want to make it easy

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.590
for you to explore new Cloud
technologies, prototype

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:24.870
applications, and do
development tasks directly

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:26.820
from your web browser.

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:29.820
Check it out at
ide.cloud.google.com.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.550
Let us know what you think, and
stay tuned for more episodes.

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:35.900
[MUSIC PLAYING]

